Usage: kill [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Send signals to processes, or list signals.

The default signal for kill is TERM. Use -l or -L/-t to list available signals.
Particularly useful signals include HUP, INT, KILL, STOP, CONT, and 0.
Alternate signals may be specified in three ways: -9, -SIGKILL or -KILL.

Note: On Windows, most signals are mapped to process termination,
except SIGTERM which attempts graceful shutdown first.

OPTIONS:
  -s, --signal ARG  specify the signal to send
  -n ARG            specify the signal to send
  -q, --queue ARG   queue an integer signal payload (unsupported on Windows)
  -l, --list[=ARG]  list signal names, or convert one signal
  -L, --table       list signal names in a table
  -t                list signal names in a table
  -f, --force       force using Win32 termination if necessary; accepted no-op
                    because WinuxCmd already uses Win32 process handles
  -W, --winpid      treat pids as Windows PIDs; accepted no-op because WinuxCmd
                    pids are already Windows PIDs
  -NUM              send signal number
  -0                check whether a process exists
  -9                send SIGKILL (force kill)
  -15               send SIGTERM (graceful termination)
  -HUP              send SIGHUP
  -SIGHUP           send SIGHUP
  -INT              send SIGINT
  -SIGINT           send SIGINT
  -QUIT             send SIGQUIT
  -SIGQUIT          send SIGQUIT
  -ILL              send SIGILL
  -SIGILL           send SIGILL
  -TRAP             send SIGTRAP
  -SIGTRAP          send SIGTRAP
  -ABRT             send SIGABRT
  -SIGABRT          send SIGABRT
  -IOT              send SIGIOT
  -SIGIOT           send SIGIOT
  -EMT              send SIGEMT
  -SIGEMT           send SIGEMT
  -FPE              send SIGFPE
  -SIGFPE           send SIGFPE
  -KILL             send SIGKILL
  -SIGKILL          send SIGKILL
  -BUS              send SIGBUS
  -SIGBUS           send SIGBUS
  -SEGV             send SIGSEGV
  -SIGSEGV          send SIGSEGV
  -SYS              send SIGSYS
  -SIGSYS           send SIGSYS
  -PIPE             send SIGPIPE
  -SIGPIPE          send SIGPIPE
  -ALRM             send SIGALRM
  -SIGALRM          send SIGALRM
  -TERM             send SIGTERM
  -SIGTERM          send SIGTERM
  -URG              send SIGURG
  -SIGURG           send SIGURG
  -STOP             send SIGSTOP
  -SIGSTOP          send SIGSTOP
  -TSTP             send SIGTSTP
  -SIGTSTP          send SIGTSTP
  -CONT             send SIGCONT
  -SIGCONT          send SIGCONT
  -CHLD             send SIGCHLD
  -SIGCHLD          send SIGCHLD
  -CLD              send SIGCLD
  -SIGCLD           send SIGCLD
  -TTIN             send SIGTTIN
  -SIGTTIN          send SIGTTIN
  -TTOU             send SIGTTOU
  -SIGTTOU          send SIGTTOU
  -IO               send SIGIO
  -SIGIO            send SIGIO
  -POLL             send SIGPOLL
  -SIGPOLL          send SIGPOLL
  -XCPU             send SIGXCPU
  -SIGXCPU          send SIGXCPU
  -XFSZ             send SIGXFSZ
  -SIGXFSZ          send SIGXFSZ
  -VTALRM           send SIGVTALRM
  -SIGVTALRM        send SIGVTALRM
  -PROF             send SIGPROF
  -SIGPROF          send SIGPROF
  -WINCH            send SIGWINCH
  -SIGWINCH         send SIGWINCH
  -PWR              send SIGPWR
  -SIGPWR           send SIGPWR
  -LOST             send SIGLOST
  -SIGLOST          send SIGLOST
  -USR1             send SIGUSR1
  -SIGUSR1          send SIGUSR1
  -USR2             send SIGUSR2
  -SIGUSR2          send SIGUSR2
  -RTMIN            send SIGRTMIN
  -SIGRTMIN         send SIGRTMIN
  -RTMAX            send SIGRTMAX
  -SIGRTMAX         send SIGRTMAX
      --help        display this help and exit
  -V, --version     output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
